.TH ANONSURF 1 "August 26, 2026" "AnonSurf 6.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anonsurf \- anonymization toolkit for Parrot Security OS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anonsurf
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B AnonSurf
manages system-wide Tor transparent routing on Parrot Security OS.
It provides commands to start and stop the AnonSurf service, inspect
runtime status, request a new Tor identity, check the public Tor status,
and manage startup integration.
.PP
Tor carries supported TCP traffic and DNS lookups, not arbitrary UDP.
UDP DNS requests use Tor's UDP-only DNSPort. TCP DNS requests to public
IPv4 resolvers use TransPort, following the same routing as other TCP traffic.
Private-network and loopback routing exceptions remain in effect.
.PP
The Go command-line interface keeps
.B anonsurf status
scriptable. Interactive Tor monitoring is available separately through
.B anonsurf monitor
or
.B anonsurf status --nyx.
.PP
Interactive lifecycle commands print the current daemon phase to standard
error while systemd records the same phase as the service status text.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --verbose
Show each operating-system command and its captured standard output and
standard error. Without this option, command output is shown only when the
command fails.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start [-k | --safe-kill]
Start system-wide Tor transparent routing. When
.B --safe-kill
is supplied, AnonSurf runs
.B /usr/lib/anonsurf/safekill
before changing service state.
.TP
.B stop [-k | --safe-kill]
Stop AnonSurf and restore the previous network settings. When
.B --safe-kill
is supplied, AnonSurf runs
.B /usr/lib/anonsurf/safekill
before changing service state.
.TP
.B restart [-k | --safe-kill]
Restart AnonSurf routing. When
.B --safe-kill
is supplied, AnonSurf runs
.B /usr/lib/anonsurf/safekill
before changing service state.
.TP
.B status
Print a read-only snapshot of AnonSurf service state, Tor service state,
DNS classification, and configured DNS servers.
.TP
.B status [-n | --nyx]
Open Nyx using the AnonSurf configuration instead of printing the
scriptable status snapshot.
.TP
.B status [--json]
Print the status snapshot as JSON. The object contains
.B anonsurf_active
and
.B tor_active
booleans,
.B dns_state,
and the
.B dns_addresses
array.
.TP
.B monitor
Open Nyx using
.B /etc/anonsurf/nyxrc.
.TP
.B changeid
Request a new Tor identity through Tor's control port.
A different exit IP is not guaranteed, and existing connections are unchanged.
.TP
.B ip [--json]
Check the public IP address used by this request and whether it is seen as Tor traffic.
The result does not verify every application's traffic.
With
.B --json,
print the
.B ip
and
.B is_tor
fields as JSON.
.TP
.B boot enable
Enable AnonSurf at system startup.
.TP
.B boot disable
Disable AnonSurf at system startup.
.TP
.B boot status
Show whether AnonSurf is enabled at startup.
.SH RELATED TOOLS
.TP
.B dnstool
Manage and inspect system DNS settings used by AnonSurf.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/anonsurf/make-torrc
Generate or restore Tor configuration from AnonSurf settings.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/resolv.conf
System DNS resolver file or symbolic link restored when AnonSurf stops.
.TP
.B /etc/anonsurf/resolv.conf.bak
Backup preserving the original resolver file or symbolic link target.
.TP
.B /var/lib/anonsurf/dns.changed
Tracks managed DNS and completed restores awaiting backup cleanup.
.TP
.B /run/anonsurf/ipv6.state
Records global and per-interface IPv6 settings for restoration.
.TP
.B /etc/anonsurf/anonsurf.cfg
AnonSurf configuration.
.TP
.B /etc/anonsurf/bridges.txt
Bridge list used when automatic bridge selection is enabled.
.TP
.B /etc/anonsurf/nyxrc
Nyx configuration used by
.B anonsurf monitor
and
.B anonsurf status --nyx.
.TP
.B /etc/tor/torrc
Tor configuration generated by AnonSurf.
.TP
.B /var/lib/anonsurf/torrc.backup
Backup of the previous Tor configuration.
